  "events": [ {
    "tStartMs": 80,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "For the last century, Canada has been one of, \nif not our single closest security, cultural,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 5040,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and economic partner. We have one of the world's \nlargest bilateral trade relationships with close  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 10400,
    "dDurationMs": 4240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to a trillion dollars of goods crisscrossing \nthis border every year, a long shared history  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 14640,
    "dDurationMs": 6560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of international cooperation, even in our less \nconsidered geopolitical excursions, and well,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 21200,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "we also just kind of like one another. Up until \nrecently, Americans reported liking Canada and  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 26880,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "spicy Canada almost as much as they liked America \nitself. On the other side of the same coin,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 32320,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Canadians had an overwhelmingly favorable opinion \nof us down here. But clearly that has changed.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 38160,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Over the last 20 months, we have proposed, \nimposed, paused, reversed, and re-implemented  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 43120,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "major tariffs and trade deals 30 separate times \nin counting. All on top of what was an existing  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 48800,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "trade deal originally negotiated by this same \nadministration. This is also not including the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 54320,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "new tariffs we put on them last week. Of course, \nas a little cherry on top, we have also threatened  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 59600,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to annex them, cut them off from vital resources, \nrename their lake, block their companies, and most  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 65040,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "egregious of all, we implied that we could beat \nthem on the ice. In a clear sign that things are  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 70240,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "getting really serious, we have also made moves \nto block their private jets to prioritize our own  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 75040,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "private jet manufacturers because, well, you know, \nthat's what's truly important in today's economy.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 80560,
    "dDurationMs": 6080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "All of this raises the obvious question of why. \nWhat possible justification could we have to do  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 86640,
    "dDurationMs": 6400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "this to a close ally? Well, we will get to that. \nBut before we do, it's also important to ask if  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 93040,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "we are even going to win this. On paper, Canada \nis being dragged unwillingly into the ring with  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 98080,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "an economy 13 times its size. But if push comes \nto shove, they could fight dirty. And there's  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 102880,
    "dDurationMs": 6880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a strong argument that we actually need them a \nlot more than they need us. Basket. We're going  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 109760,
    "dDurationMs": 4560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to stop all negotiations with Canada right now \nuntil they straighten out their act. Companies  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 114320,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in Canada are going to start looking elsewhere. \nThe president says no more selling bombardier in  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 120080,
    "dDurationMs": 6720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the United States. President Trump officially \nrenamed Lake Ontario Lake America. President  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 126800,
    "dDurationMs": 4400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Trump can can call it whatever he wants, but I \ncan tell you the rest of the world will always  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 131200,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "call it Lake Ontario. Canada's automotive \nsector is buckling up and bracing for impact.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 136080,
    "dDurationMs": 6400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the US introduced at the last uh hours in the last \nhours um efforts to restrict our ability to have  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 142480,
    "dDurationMs": 6800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "other trade deals. A deal we would not accept. \nOkay, so we are kind of doing a lot at the moment,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 149280,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "but we should probably break down some of these \njustifications, however loose they may be, because  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 154640,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "even if we assume that these orders won't just get \nstruck down again, and even if we assume tariffs  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 159360,
    "dDurationMs": 4400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "worked as intended in the first place, and even \nif we assume that Canada has always secretly been  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 163760,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "some antagonistic trade sheet that nobody has been \nable to see before, now there are still some major  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 168720,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "problems with how we are um fixing this situation. \nThe first is that we don't live in the Middle  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 174480,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Ages anymore where mercantileist style trade was a \nzero- sum game and the winner was whoever exported  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 179840,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the most and hoarded the most gold. We do indeed \nhave a trade deficit with Canada, meaning they  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 184800,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "sell more to us than we sell to them. But even \nthough the word deficit kind of sounds scary,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 190080,
    "dDurationMs": 4480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "it's not like Canada is just sending us shipping \ncontainers full of finished goods while we sit  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 194560,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "around slowly bleeding cash to them. A lot of this \ntrade bounces back across the border several times  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 199440,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and it overwhelmingly ends up going to American \nfactories rather than American consumers. The  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 204400,
    "dDurationMs": 4000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Wall Street Journal chased one of these supply \nchains and found that a single component starts  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 208400,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "as raw aluminum shipped in from Quebec to Michigan \nbefore it goes back up to Ontario to be cast and  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 213840,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "pre-machined, returns to the US for finishing, \ntravels to Mexico for further processing,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 218640,
    "dDurationMs": 4560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Wisconsin for assembly, and ends up back in \nMichigan for installation into an engine.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 223200,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "That engine is then put into a truck which in \nmany cases is sold back to Canada. These supply  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 228400,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "chains cross the border multiple times over which \nmeans a blanket tariff on every crossing compounds  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 233520,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "this cost over and over again making this kind \nof manufacturing untenable. Now maybe there is  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 239440,
    "dDurationMs": 6080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the argument that this is kind of the point. Maybe \nwe could bring all of these steps and in turn jobs  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 245520,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "back into America if we just made crossing the \nborder financially unviable. But even assuming  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 251200,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that was possible for incredibly complex modern \nsupply chains, it would still likely end up being  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 256080,
    "dDurationMs": 4640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a lot less competitive. A company like Ford \ndoesn't send its engine components on a grand  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 260720,
    "dDurationMs": 4480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "tour of the world for kicks. It does it because \ncertain places have advantages in performing a  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 265200,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "certain task. Quebec, for example, smelts a lot of \naluminum because that is a highly energyintensive  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 270480,
    "dDurationMs": 6160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "operation and they have a lot of relatively cheap \nhydroelectric energy. across this border. Yes,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 276640,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Canada does send more goods this way than we do \nto them, but the actual margin compared to the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 281840,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "overall volume is very small. We are also a bigger \nmarket and we are still arguably getting the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 287760,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "better end of the deal for one simple reason. It \nmight be harder to visualize in a simple mind, but  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 293360,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "not all international trade is stuff loaded into a \nshipping container. A good chunk of our bilateral  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 298560,
    "dDurationMs": 6320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "trade with Canada is services. We actually have \na large services trade surplus with Canada of  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 304880,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "roughly $28 billion a year, which in plain English \nmeans they pay us a lot more for things like  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 310560,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "financial work, consulting, technology usage, and \nintellectual property than we pay to them. This  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 316480,
    "dDurationMs": 4240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "is important because selling these services is \nnormally much more lucrative than selling actual  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 320720,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "physical items. The margin on manufactured goods \nhas to account for the cost of materials, energy,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 326160,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "man-hour, tooling, shipping, and wastage. So the \nactual profit at the end is usually very thin.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 332160,
    "dDurationMs": 4000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "On the other hand, something like a consulting \ncontract from a firm in New York to a business in  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 336160,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Ontario can make a $100,000 a month with the only \nactual expense being the salary of an overworked  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 342000,
    "dDurationMs": 4640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "junior analyst with a claude subscription. So \neven though the raw numbers of what we are sending  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 346640,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "across the border may be slightly in their favor, \nif you look at this purely as a two-way street,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 351360,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the margins on the stuff that we are sending them \nare far creamier than what they are sending back.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 356560,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So even with just a surface level breakdown \nof this relationship, blowing it up over any  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 361520,
    "dDurationMs": 6560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "perceived inequity sounds pretty dumb. But it gets \ndumber because so far we have been assuming that  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 368080,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the USA and Canada are the only two countries \nin the world. But after some rigorous research,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 372880,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the team and I have actually discovered that \nthey're not. So it's time to learn how many works  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 378160,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to find out how we are getting much more out of \nCanada than they are getting out of us. For now,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 384080,
    "dDurationMs": 3680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "this video is sponsored by Monarch. If you \nhave ever tried to track your money across  "
    } ]
  }, {
