    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "multiple apps and accounts, you know how easy \nit is to lose sight of the big picture. Monarch  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 392560,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "is a private ad-free personal finance platform \nthat syncs all your accounts from 13,000 plus  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 397520,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "institutions into one clean dashboard. What I \nlike about Monarch is that it does not try to  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 402480,
    "dDurationMs": 4160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "make you obsess over every transaction. It \nseparates your fixed and flexible spending,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 406640,
    "dDurationMs": 4400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "keeps everything organized with your own rules and \ncategories, and when something unusual happens,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 411040,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "it explains what changed in plain English. \nYou stay aware without being overwhelmed. My  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 416160,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "partner and I actually use the shared view for our \nmonthly check-ins. We both see the same dashboard,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 420960,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the same goals, the same recurring bills. It has \ncut out most of the awkward money conversations  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 425920,
    "dDurationMs": 4400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "because there are no surprises. Monarch \nalso tracks your spending, credit score,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 430320,
    "dDurationMs": 4400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and net worth over time and gives you clear \npayoff timelines for savings and paying down  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 434720,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "debt. So you can see whether you are actually \nmaking progress instead of just hoping you are.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 439440,
    "dDurationMs": 3920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And because you are the customer and not \nthe product, Monarch is completely adree  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 443360,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "with bank level encryption. No ads and no data \nselling. Try Monarch yourself. Scan the QR code  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 449120,
    "dDurationMs": 10480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "on screen or use the link in my description and \nuse code HMW50 to get 50% off your first year."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 459600,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Okay. So, so far this has all been ignoring the \nsmall little fact that just looking at the US  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 464800,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Canada trade relationship in complete isolation \nof anything else is narrow-minded at best. The  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 470720,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "manufacturing supply chain hoke pokey of putting \nmachined components in and taking your finished  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 475440,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "components out is close to just being a wash in \neither direction. The bulk of the trade surplus  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 480480,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that Canada maintains with us actually comes from \nthe fact that they ship a lot of crude oil from  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 485360,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "their sands into America through these pipelines. \nEven before the special military operation in  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 490400,
    "dDurationMs": 6720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Iran, Canada already accounted for roughly 60% of \nall US crude oil imports. For Midwest refineries,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 497120,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "roughly 73% of their crude comes from Canadian \npipelines. That oil gets refined on the Gulf  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 502560,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Coast and in the Midwest into diesel, plastics, \nand gas. And then we go out and sell it at a  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 507520,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "markup to our domestic market, which you know \ncould use reliable oil supplies right now. We  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 512720,
    "dDurationMs": 3760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "also make a lot of money by reselling \nthese refined products internationally,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 516480,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "including back to Canada itself. If we take out \nthese oil imports, the USA actually has a trade  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 522240,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "surplus with Canada. But the bigger issue here \nis that we are getting a ready supply of raw  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 527680,
    "dDurationMs": 4640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "material that we can then sell at a nice healthy \nmarkup. This would kind of be like a supermarket  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 532320,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "complaining about running a trade deficit with \na farmer. Yes, technically we buy more off them  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 537120,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "than they buy off us, but our business model kind \nof depends on getting those supplies in the first  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 542080,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "place. This is also ignoring the small little \ndetail that our strategic petroleum reserve is  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 547120,
    "dDurationMs": 6560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "sitting at around 287 million barrels, the lowest \nit has been since 1982. So, it would probably be  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 553680,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "prudent to keep our largest supplier happy in the \nmeantime. America is also already the largest oil  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 559040,
    "dDurationMs": 4240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "producer on the planet. So, it's not exactly \nlike we could claim that Canadian imports are  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 563280,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "undercutting our domestic industry. Now, for what \nit's worth, we have made carveout exceptions for  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 568400,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "oil in most of these proposed tariffs because, \nwell, uh, we kind of realize we need it. But the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 573600,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "reason why Canada sends so much of its oil to \nus in the first place is because it's easy and  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 578720,
    "dDurationMs": 4320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "economically viable. They have invested heavily \ninto pipeline infrastructure to effectively  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 583040,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "handfeed us cheap energy. because we are a \nmassive market that is geographically close,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 588080,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "but also because for a long time we have long been \na reliable purchaser. These trade disruptions mean  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 593680,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "any new major investments are naturally being \nrethought. Canada recently expanded its Trans  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 599120,
    "dDurationMs": 6240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Mountain pipeline from 300,000 to almost 900,000 \nbarrels per day. And since that expansion, non-  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 605360,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "US Canadian oil exports have more than tripled \nwith Asia projected to take as much as 70% of  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 610640,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the pipelines's capacity going forward. Canadian \nleaders are also now pushing to develop their own  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 615520,
    "dDurationMs": 4480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "refining capacity so that they can add value to \ntheir own supply chain rather than relying on a  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 620000,
    "dDurationMs": 6240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "partner that is to put it diplomatically clearly \nunreliable. In the past, yeah, sure, they were  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 626240,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "kind of putting all of their export eggs into one \nbasket, but the basket was so good it just didn't  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 631760,
    "dDurationMs": 4640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "make economic sense to diversify into shipping oil \nacross the ocean when it could be piped across the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 636400,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "land border. Today, it would be kind of dumb not \nto make sure there were other options available.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 641680,
    "dDurationMs": 6320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It sounds bad, but it gets worse. Even outside of \noil, there are a lot of raw materials that we get  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 648000,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "from Canada that would be really hard to get from \nanywhere else. Pot ash has been a big one. Again,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 653920,
    "dDurationMs": 3920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "this is one of those component materials \nthat should mean nothing to most people,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 657840,
    "dDurationMs": 4400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "but we are now finding out about it because \nit turns out it's kind of important. This is  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 662240,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a mineral used for agricultural fertilizers, which \nis then used to grow the food that my food eats.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 667680,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The US currently imports about 93% of the pod \nash it needs with Canada supplying roughly 80% of  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 673280,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that. This is a natural resource that depends on \ngeological conditions which in plain English means  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 678640,
    "dDurationMs": 4080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "all of the tariffs in the world aren't going to \nmagically create deposits of the same stuff here  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 682720,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "at home. We are also dependent on them for uranium \nwhere they cover about a third of our civilian  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 687600,
    "dDurationMs": 6240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "nuclear fuel supply and aluminum where they supply \naround 68% of our imports largely from Quebec  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 693840,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "using that cheap hydroelectric power from earlier. \nNow, once again, Podash was specifically exempted  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 699280,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "from our latest round of tariffs and a random \nselection of the other 30 proposals as well.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 704000,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But this alone is kind of a tacid admission that \nwe kind of need these resources to keep flowing.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 709120,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But if we keep antagonizing our main supplier of \nmaterials that we cannot produce at home, well,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 714080,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Canada has been fairly restrained so far. But \nto use the parlance of international diplomacy,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 720080,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "they do have a few cards to play. They've already \nhit us with over $20 billion worth of retaliatory  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 725920,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "tariffs, pulled US goods out of their government \npurchasing, and the import bans on dairy, alcohol,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 730880,
    "dDurationMs": 4640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and motorcycles are being met with their own \nrestrictions going the other way. Ontario has  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 735520,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "slapped a 25% search charge on electricity exports \nto Michigan, Minnesota, and New York. Power that  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 741200,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "currently supplies roughly 1.5 million American \nhomes and businesses. And on top of this, if we  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 746480,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "remember that little ongoing bond crisis, Canadian \ninstitutions hold around $460 billion worth of  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 752240,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "US Treasury securities, which means if things got \nreally ugly, they could start dumping those bonds,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 757360,
    "dDurationMs": 4320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "driving up our borrowing costs beyond the \nrecord levels they are already at. Yes,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 761680,
    "dDurationMs": 4400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "we are a much bigger economy that should in theory \nbe able to throw our weight around. But we have  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 766080,
    "dDurationMs": 4320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "kind of shot ourselves in the foot several times \nbefore picking this particular fight and stepping  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 770400,
    "dDurationMs": 6240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in the ring to an opponent that we are handcuffed \nto. All right. Now, like most good fairy tales,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 776640,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "there is a hint of truth in the fact that yes, \nAmerican manufacturing has been hit by increased  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 781920,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "global pressure, particularly from new competitors \nin Asia, largely Japan in the 80s and '90s and  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 787120,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "China today. It's also not completely unfair to \nsay that a lot of these trade imbalances have come  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 792000,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "from other countries using some slightly unfair \neconomic tactics, but Canada is really not part  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 797280,
    "dDurationMs": 4640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of that group. Part of the justification for these \nmost recent attacks on Canada has been that they  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 801920,
    "dDurationMs": 4240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "are manipulating their currency to make their \nexports artificially more competitive. Again,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 806160,
    "dDurationMs": 4400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "an accusation that has been leveled against \nemerging competitors in Asia. Now, ignoring  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 810560,
    "dDurationMs": 4560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "for a second the small fact that Canada has a free \nfloating currency that hasn't been intervened in  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 815120,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "since the 1990s, which is arguably more than we \ncan say about our own, even if this was true,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 820800,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "this would kind of be a good thing for us. Our \nreal competition for global industry is a country  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 825840,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "like China. And yes, they do have real competitive \nadvantages like lower wages, government backing,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 831360,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and looser environmental standards. And yes, \nthey do also influence their currency to make  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 836080,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "their exports more competitive. But the thing is, \ntaking this out on Canada is really only going to  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 841200,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "make things worse. This would kind of be like if \nwe were running a shop and a bigger, cheaper shop  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 845920,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "moved into town and started undercutting us. Our \nreaction right now is for some reason to attack  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 851200,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "our suppliers. What's more, if Canada actually \nwas influencing their currency, which again they  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 856560,
    "dDurationMs": 4640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "are not, this would kind of be like our supplier \ngiving us a discount on our inputs, which would  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 861200,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "be a good thing. If this other player really is a \nlong-term threat to our economic business model,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 866000,
    "dDurationMs": 4000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the real pro gamer move would actually be to \nstrengthen the relationship we have with our  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 870000,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "upstream suppliers and downstream customers \nto squeeze them out. Just ask anybody around  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 875200,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "this table. This is anti-competitive business \n101. You cuddle your customers and court your  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 880640,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "suppliers to make it financially irresponsible \nfor either of them to leave to a competitor.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 885360,
    "dDurationMs": 4640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "While this is in theory illegal for businesses \nto do within the country, it's much harder to  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 890000,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "enforce internationally. But instead of using our \nmonopsy and power to strengthen our relationships,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 895280,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "we are alienating them and somewhat predictably \npushing them straight towards Beijing. Another  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 900800,
    "dDurationMs": 4240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "variable that's worth keeping in mind during \nall of this is that most Canadians see all of  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 905040,
    "dDurationMs": 4560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "this as an attack on them. Whereas most average \nAmericans really don't understand why we are doing  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 909600,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "this to our northern brothers. This means that on \naverage, Canadians are going to be willing to put  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 914320,
    "dDurationMs": 4080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "up with a higher degree of economic suffering \nthan the American households who largely don't  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 918400,
    "dDurationMs": 4000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "understand why we are fighting our friends in the \nfirst place. Now, I know this might sound a bit  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 922400,
    "dDurationMs": 4560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "wishy-washy, but we are both still democracies \nand there's a much better chance of them voting  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 926960,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to keep this fight going longer than we do. 41% \nof Canadians now view the United States as an  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 932560,
    "dDurationMs": 6160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "enemy. 73% support their government's retaliatory \ntariffs against us. And it's not like Americans  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 938720,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "are particularly excited about this either. \n57% of us oppose tariffs on Canada. Normally,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 944560,
    "dDurationMs": 4480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "this is where I would try to play devil's advocate \nto somehow justify these actions. Normally,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 949040,
    "dDurationMs": 4400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the best way to understand a debate is to try \nand argue in favor of the side you don't agree  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 953440,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "with. But in this case, I'm afraid that would \nkind of just end up sanewashing something that  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 958320,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "really is just logically indefensible. But in \nthe interest of making sure nobody can say I  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 963280,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "am a one-sided hack if you somewhat ironically \nswitch to Klingan in the player and go back to  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 968400,
    "dDurationMs": 3680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the start of this video, I have tried my very \nbest to present the counterarguments to play  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 972080,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "devil's advocate as best as I can. But if you \nunderstandably aren't interested in hearing me  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 977040,
    "dDurationMs": 4160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "try to be a spin doctor, go and watch this video \nnext to find out why all of this hasn't caused  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 981200,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "an economic crisis yet. And don't forget to like \nand subscribe to keep on learning how money works."
    } ]
  } ]
}
